## 6. Azure CLI

### The pattern
```
az  <service>  <thing>  <action>  --flags
```

| Part | Examples |
|---|---|
| service | `group`, `network`, `vm` |
| thing | `vnet`, `vnet subnet`, `nsg`, `nsg rule` |
| action | `create`, `list`, `show`, `delete`, `update`, `start`, `stop` |

- Things inside something else need the parent's name: subnets need `--vnet-name`; rules need `--nsg-name`
- Help: add `--help` to any command (see **Examples** at the bottom), or `az find "what I want"`

### Common flags

| Flag | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `-g` / `--resource-group` | Which folder |
| `--name` / `-n` | What to call it, or which one to act on |
| `--location` / `-l` | Region (e.g., `westus2`) |
| `-o table` | Readable table instead of JSON |
| `--query "..."` | Pick only certain fields (JMESPath) |
| `--yes` | Skip "are you sure?" |
| `-d` (on `vm list`) | Add IPs and power state |

### Commands

```bash
# ---------- Resource groups ----------
az group create --name rg1 --location westus2     # Make a folder called rg1 in West US 2
az group list -o table                            # Show all folders
az group delete --name rg1 --yes                  # Delete the folder AND everything in it

# ---------- VNets ----------
az network vnet create -g rg1 --name vnet1 --address-prefixes 10.0.0.0/16
                                                  # Make a VNet with 65,536 addresses
az network vnet create -g rg1 --name vnet1 --address-prefixes 10.0.0.0/16 \
  --subnet-name subnet1 --subnet-prefixes 10.0.1.0/24
                                                  # Make a VNet and its first subnet in one go
az network vnet list -g rg1 -o table              # Show all VNets
az network vnet delete -g rg1 --name vnet1        # Delete a VNet (fails if VMs use it)

# ---------- Subnets ----------
az network vnet subnet create -g rg1 --vnet-name vnet1 --name subnet1 --address-prefixes 10.0.1.0/24
                                                  # Add a subnet to a VNet
az network vnet subnet list -g rg1 --vnet-name vnet1 -o table
                                                  # Show all subnets in a VNet
az network vnet subnet list -g rg1 --vnet-name vnet1 \
  --query "[].{Name:name, Range:addressPrefix}" -o table
                                                  # Show subnet names + ranges
                                                  # (use addressPrefixes[0] for portal-created subnets)
az network vnet subnet delete -g rg1 --vnet-name vnet1 --name subnet1
                                                  # Remove a subnet (fails if anything is in it)

# ---------- VMs ----------
az vm create -g rg1 --name vm1 --image Ubuntu2204 --vnet-name vnet1 --subnet subnet1 \
  --admin-username azureuser --generate-ssh-keys
                                                  # Build an Ubuntu VM with an SSH key.
                                                  # Auto-creates its NSG, network card, disk and public IP.
#   --size Standard_B2ats_v2                      # Choose the VM size
#   --public-ip-address ""                        # No public IP (private only)

az vm list -g rg1 -d -o table                     # Show VMs with IPs and running state
az vm list -g rg1 -d --query "[].{Name:name, PrivateIP:privateIps}" -o table
                                                  # Show VM names + private IPs
az vm stop -g rg1 --name vm1                      # Turn off (disk still costs a little)
az vm start -g rg1 --name vm1                     # Turn back on
az vm delete -g rg1 --name vm1 --yes              # Delete permanently

# ---------- NSGs ----------
az network nsg create -g rg1 --name nsg1          # Make an NSG (only default rules)
az network nsg list -g rg1 -o table               # Show all NSGs
az network nsg rule create -g rg1 --nsg-name nsg1 --name rule1 --priority 100 \
  --direction Inbound --access Deny --protocol Icmp \
  --source-address-prefixes 10.0.1.0/24 --destination-address-prefixes '*' --destination-port-ranges '*'
                                                  # Add a rule: block incoming pings from 10.0.1.0/24
az network nsg rule list -g rg1 --nsg-name nsg1 --include-default -o table
                                                  # Show all rules, including built-in ones
az network nsg rule delete -g rg1 --nsg-name nsg1 --name rule1
                                                  # Remove a rule

# ---------- Connect and test ----------
ssh azureuser@<public-ip>                         # Log in to a VM
ping -c 4 <private-ip>                            # (inside a VM) Test reachability
exit                                              # Leave the VM

# ---------- Lost SSH key ----------
ssh-keygen -t rsa -b 4096 -f ~/.ssh/id_rsa -N ""  # Make a new key pair
az vm user update -g rg1 --name vm1 --username azureuser --ssh-key-value ~/.ssh/id_rsa.pub
                                                  # Put the new public key on the VM
```

### Good practice
- **Predict, then run.** Guess the result first, then check
- **Read errors for the `Code:` line**, not the traceback
- **Verify after every change** with a `list` command
- **Delete the resource group at the end of every session**, then confirm with `az group list -o table`
